c0bdef648babb1ac,2026-11-16T13:51:31+00:00,meeting,upcoming,El Dorado County,Legistar,El Dorado County,,Board of Supervisors,2026-11-17,Attachment: Public Comment BOS Rcvd 11-13-26.pdf,https://eldorado.legistar1.com/eldorado/attachments/8df6200e-6ba3-4237-b0e1-c0f17ec99c23.pdf,https://eldorado.legistar1.com/eldorado/attachments/8df6200e-6ba3-4237-b0e1-c0f17ec99c23.pdf#page=2,2,false,0,short-term rental; vacation rental; ADU; parking,22,Long Range; Transportation,agenda,Agenda item,Agenda item; Public comment,"access and activity differently. That does not mean impacts should be ignored. It means regulations should be proportionate to the actual circumstances of the property. We support reasonable,…",5,true,"...HRs would be an emormous burden and loss. Residents should not have to sacrifice the safety and quiet enjoyment of their homes to accommodate an expansion of short-term rentals. I urge the Board to reject these changes. Sincerely, Janet Barbieri Placerville Public Comment BOS Rcvd 11/13/26 26-1805 #30",2,false,"[{""type"": ""Agenda item"", ""title"": ""access and activity differently. That does not mean impacts should be ignored. It means regulations should be proportionate to the actual circumstances of the property. We support reasonable,…"", ""pages"": [5], ""count"": 1, ""in_title"": true}, {""type"": ""Agenda item"", ""title"": ""1. Increasing the Number of Guests Will Increase Impacts on Rural Communities Allowing larger groups to occupy VHRs will inevitably increase the potential for noise, traffic, parking congestion,…"", ""pages"": [8], ""count"": 1, ""in_title"": true}, {""type"": ""Agenda item"", ""title"": ""5. Permanent Residents Should Not Bear the Costs of Expanding Commercial Vacation Rentals Residents who have invested in homes and property in rural El Dorado County should not be expected to absorb…"", ""pages"": [10], ""count"": 2, ""in_title"": true}, {""type"": ""Agenda item"", ""title"": ""6. Preserve the Intent of Existing Protections The existing 500-foot separation requirement is a reasonable protective measure intended to prevent VHR concentration and reduce potential conflicts…"", ""pages"": [10], ""count"": 1, ""in_title"": true}, {""type"": ""Agenda item"", ""title"": ""1. Reject the recommendation to increase the maximum occupancy of VHRs where such increases could intensify noise, traffic, parking, infrastructure, and public safety impacts"", ""pages"": [11], ""count"": 1, ""in_title"": true}, {""type"": ""Agenda item"", ""title"": ""2. Retain the existing 500-foot separation requirement between VHRs on parcels of five acres or larger to prevent the clustering and overconcentration of vacation rentals in rura I residential areas"", ""pages"": [11], ""count"": 1, ""in_title"": true}, {""type"": ""Agenda item"", ""title"": ""~E C:L DORADO COUNTY FARM BUREAU 2460 Headington Road Placerville. CA 95667-5216 Phone: 530.622.7773 Fax: 530.622.7839 Email: lnfo@edcfb.com Attention: Board of Supervisors November 13, 2026 RE:…"", ""pages"": [4], ""count"": 2, ""in_title"": false}, {""type"": ""Agenda item"", ""title"": ""We do, however, recommend careful review of the provisions characterized as clean-up or clarification. Several could have consequences beyond technical clarification, depending on how they are…"", ""pages"": [6], ""count"": 1, ""in_title"": false}, {""type"": ""Agenda item"", ""title"": ""Opposition to Recommended Changes to Vacation Home Rental Regulations (Agenda Item 26- 1805) From Scott Matyac <jsmatyac@outJook.com> Date Fri 11/13/2026 3:24 PM To BOS-Clerk of the Board…"", ""pages"": [8], ""count"": 2, ""in_title"": false}, {""type"": ""Agenda item"", ""title"": ""Increasing the permitted number of guests will place additional pressure on these neighborhoods and create greater opportunities for conflicts between transient visitors and permanent residents. More…"", ""pages"": [9], ""count"": 2, ""in_title"": false}, {""type"": ""Agenda item"", ""title"": ""These impacts are not necessarily confined to the individual property hosting the rental. They affect neighboring properties and the broader community. The County should evaluate the cumulative…"", ""pages"": [10], ""count"": 2, ""in_title"": false}, {""type"": ""Agenda item"", ""title"": ""6. Preserve the rural residential character and quality of life that make the West Slope of El Dorado County a desirable place to live. Conclusion The issue before the Board is bigger than whether…"", ""pages"": [11], ""count"": 1, ""in_title"": false}, {""type"": ""Agenda item"", ""title"": ""Rather than eliminating this safeguard, the County should preserve it and evaluate whether additional protections are needed to address the cumulative impacts of increased occupancy. If the County is…"", ""pages"": [11], ""count"": 1, ""in_title"": false}, {""type"": ""Public comment"", ""title"": ""Public Comment to BOS on 11/17 - Vacation Home Rental ordinance changes From Janet Barbieri <janet.m.barbieri@att.net> Date Fri 11/13/2026 3:05 PM To BOS-Clerk of the Board <edc.cob@edcgov.us> Cc…"", ""pages"": [2], ""count"": 4, ""in_title"": false}]","[{""term"": ""short-term rental"", ""page"": 2, ""text"": ""...HRs would be an emormous burden and loss. Residents should not have to sacrifice the safety and quiet enjoyment of their homes to accommodate an expansion of short-term rentals. I urge the Board to reject these changes. Sincerely, Janet Barbieri Placerville Public Comment BOS Rcvd 11/13/26 26-1805 #30""}, {""term"": ""short-term rental"", ""page"": 4, ""text"": ""...his is particularly important when considering the differences between rural working lands and smaller residential neighborhoods. The concerns associated with short-term rentals in densely developed areas such as Meyers and the Tahoe Basin should not automatically determine policy for a five-to-200-acre agricultural or rural parcel o...""}, {""term"": ""vacation rental"", ""page"": 2, ""text"": ""...do not mean homes are far apart. In fact, I can see and hear many houses in my vicinity-we are not that far apart. Eliminating the buffer invites clusters of vacation rentals in established residential neighborhoods, affecting noise, traffic and a loss of community cohesion. • Do not increase occupancy limits. Two guests per bedro...""}, {""term"": ""vacation rental"", ""page"": 2, ""text"": ""...• Protect rural roads and evacuation routes. Our neighborhoods have narrow roads, limited access, and significant wildfire exposure. Increasing the number of vacation rentals introduces more vehicles and visitors unfamiliar with local conditions. • Consider cumulative impacts. The County must evaluate the combined effects of multi...""}, {""term"": ""vacation rental"", ""page"": 2, ""text"": ""...oduces more vehicles and visitors unfamiliar with local conditions. • Consider cumulative impacts. The County must evaluate the combined effects of multiple vacation rentals on a neighborhood, rather than approving each property in isolation. Five-acre parcels are not isolated estates, and rural residential neighborhoods are not c...""}, {""term"": ""ADU"", ""page"": 6, ""text"": ""...ual provisions of the VHR ordinance. The County needs a coordinated rural lodging framework that recognizes the differences among VHRs, agricultural lodging, ADUs, ranch marketing, guest accommodations, and low-impact camping, while identifying where these uses complement one another and where distinct standards are ne...""}, {""term"": ""parking"", ""page"": 4, ""text"": ""...omatically determine policy for a five-to-200-acre agricultural or rural parcel on the West Slope. Larger parcels can offer greater separation, more room for parking, and opportunities to manage ll P a ge Public Comment BOS Rcvd 11/13/26 26-1805 #30""}, {""term"": ""parking"", ""page"": 5, ""text"": ""...ations should be proportionate to the actual circumstances of the property. We support reasonable, enforceable standards addressing occupancy, noise, traffic, parking, fire access, water and septic capacity, lighting, and trash. The objective should be to regulate demonstrable impacts rather than presume that a lawful visi...""}, {""term"": ""parking"", ""page"": 8, ""text"": ""...Number of Guests Will Increase Impacts on Rural Communities Allowing larger groups to occupy VHRs will inevitably increase the potential for noise, traffic, parking congestion, trash, outdoor activity, and disturbances to neighboring residents. Rural residential properties are not hotels or commercial event venues. They...""}]"
