 with the conditions at Safe Camp, stating that the ba...""}, {""term"": ""sidewalk"", ""page"": 18, ""text"": ""...of $498,007.00]; and approve a separate project contingency fund [in the amount of $50,000.00]. The Project will construct approximately 1,000 linear feet of sidewalk in Sun Valley, Nevada from Mineral Avenue to Quartz Lane providing a safer, more accessible pedestrian route for residents and students near Bennett Element...""}, {""term"": ""parking"", ""page"": 5, ""text"": ""...nd them, Mr. McDonald stated that a super center was simply a larger voting center and nothing more. He detailed that a super center had more resources, more parking, voter check- in stations, more ballot marking devices (BMDs), additional ballot scanners, and staff to assist""}, {""term"": ""parking"", ""page"": 6, ""text"": ""...McDonald shared that they would post results as vote centers came back with their results. He explained that the ROV’s Office would be expanding out into the parking garage, which he felt had worked well for the last primary election. He shared that, as the Board was likely aware, the ROV ’s Office was getting a new wareh...""}, {""term"": ""parking"", ""page"": 6, ""text"": ""...ROV ’s Office was getting a new warehouse and would have plenty of space at that time to expand and scale their operations, but for now, they had to use the parking garage just to intake ballots and supplies on election night. He detailed that as the 41 voting locations returned results on election night, the ROV’s Offic...""}, {""term"": ""stormwater"", ""page"": 11, ""text"": ""...ears 2027 -2031, between Washoe County and the Nevada Division of Environmental Protection to continue Washoe County’s commitment to participate in an urban stormwater pollutant load reduction program (Program) at Lake Tahoe. The primary goal of the Program is to construct and manage the required infrastructure to reduce th...""}, {""term"": ""stormwater"", ""page"": 17, ""text"": ""...Water Quality Improvement Project [in the amount of $179,157.82] to support the Lake Tahoe Maximum Daily Load requirements to reduce the pollutant load in stormwater runoff; and (3) direct the Comptroller’s Office to make the necessary budget amendments. Community Services. (Commission District 1.) There was no respo...""}, {""term"": ""water quality"", ""page"": 11, ""text"": ""...oe Regional Planning Agency Mitigation Funds to provide financial support for the planning, project management, permitting, and design of the Burnt Cedar Water Quality Improvement Project. Community Services. (Commission District 1.) 26-0682 8C2 Recommendation to approve the Interlocal Agreement to Implement the Lake T...""}, {""term"": ""water quality"", ""page"": 17, ""text"": ""...acknowledged. 26-0704 AGENDA ITEM 11 Recommendation to: (1) Approve Amendment #1 to the Project Funding Agreement for the Upper Third & Rosewood Creeks Water Quality Improvement Project (FTPLR 24- 001) [increasing the total award by $1,024,309.82 to the amount of $1,766,767.91 with a Washoe County cash/in-kind match in t...""}, {""term"": ""water quality"", ""page"": 17, ""text"": ""...match in the amount of $411,691.75 for the project] for the purpose of planning, permitting, design, and construction of the Upper Third and Rosewood Creeks Water Quality Improvement Project, with provisions to receive additional funding for future portions of the project; (2) accept the Upper Third and Rosewood Creeks Water Q...""}]"
