ng Tahoe Transportation District meeting Mayor Pro Tem Roberts 1. Attended South Tahoe Transit JPA Mayor Bass 1. Issued key to the city to Howie Nave 2. Encouraged voting on Election Day Adjournment Mayor Bass adjourned the meeting at 2:13 p.m....""}, {""term"": ""transit"", ""page"": 41, ""text"": ""...the area between the east curb line of Lake Tahoe Boulevard from the intersection of Heavenly Village Way to the south curb line (or prolongation thereof) of Transit Way; the south curb line of Transit Way from the intersection of Lake Tahoe Boulevard to the west curbline of Bellamy Court; the west curbline of Bellamy Cou...""}, {""term"": ""transit"", ""page"": 41, ""text"": ""...ansit Way from the intersection of Lake Tahoe Boulevard to the west curbline of Bellamy Court; the west curbline of Bellamy Court from the south curb line of Transit Way to the north curb line of Heavenly Village Way; and the north curbline of Heavenly Village Way from the intersection of Bellamy Court to the intersection...""}, {""term"": ""parking"", ""page"": 5, ""text"": ""...BPR Consulting Group, 4Leaf Inc., TRB and Associates and Urban 37 for Plan Review, Permit Technician and Inspection Services 15. Establishment of No Overnight Parking in all Municipal Off-Street Parking Lots 16. City Code of Ethics Update 17. Amendment #1 to Professional Services Agreement with Best Best & Krieger LLP to Pro...""}, {""term"": ""parking"", ""page"": 5, ""text"": ""...ocx 02-PSA-4Leaf Inc.docx 03-PSA-BPR_Group.docx 04-TRB-TRB.docx 05-PSA-Urban37.docx Requested Action / Suggested Motions: Pass a Resolution establishing No Parking from 11 pm to 6 am with tow-away authority for all municipal off-street parking lots. Responsible Staff Member(s): Mohammad Qureshi, Director of Public Works I...""}, {""term"": ""parking"", ""page"": 5, ""text"": ""...l off-street parking lots. Responsible Staff Member(s): Mohammad Qureshi, Director of Public Works Item 15 - Executive Summary.pdf 01-Staff_Report_No overnight parking in City Parking Lots-clean.docx 02-Resolution_Establish no overnight parking in City Lots.docx Requested Action / Suggested Motions: Pass a Resolution amend...""}, {""term"": ""shoreline"", ""page"": 365, ""text"": ""...Revetment and Improvements Project (Project), the City removed the plastic super sack sandbags that had been previously used as a revetment measure along the shoreline in 2018. Because the super sacks were exposed to sun, wind, wave action, and physical wear over time, their condition raised questions regarding the potential...""}, {""term"": ""shoreline"", ""page"": 365, ""text"": ""...gineered function beneath the rock protection system. Geotextile materials can provide separation, filtration, stabilization, and erosion control benefits in shoreline and infrastructure applications. However, where such materials become exposed, damaged, or displaced, they may warrant inspection, removal, or other managemen...""}, {""term"": ""shoreline"", ""page"": 377, ""text"": ""...ay work for some temporary erosion control uses, but not all. Prioritize exposed, frayed, displaced, submerged, or temporary materials near drainage systems, shorelines, and SEZs. Key Take-away: Focus resources on materials most likely to fragment and reach sensitive waterways. 377""}, {""term"": ""scenic"", ""page"": 415, ""text"": ""...rve trees that will provide shade to structures, especially on southern exposures.  Screening: Retain vegetation that will provide screening from designated scenic corridors.  Wildlife Benefits: Retain trees that provide wildlife benefits such as food, thermal or visual cover, and nesting.  Growing Space: Provide...""}, {""term"": ""defensible space"", ""page"": 352, ""text"": ""...ire-wise landscapes. The Project will pair incentives with Water Efficiency Technician consultations and education on drought-resilient and fire-wise plants, defensible space, efficient irrigation, and landscape practices that support both water conservation and wildfire resilience. Envt 1 0 TINS $ 12,339.08 TI...""}, {""term"": ""defensible space"", ""page"": 415, ""text"": ""...ys; consequently, steep slopes are more susceptible to erosion and create a greater challenge for retention and preservation of vegetation.  Integrate fire-defensible space measures into plans for vegetation retention. See BMP Handbook Chapter 5, Soil and Vegetation Management for more information.  Species: Conserve the repre...""}, {""term"": ""defensible space"", ""page"": 423, ""text"": ""...or more of the following methods to control dust:  Install temporary/permanent vegetation and organic mulch. Ensure application is in accordance with fire defensible space specifications as outlined in Living with Fire: A Guide for the Homeowner, Lake Tahoe Basin Edition and Chapter 5 Soil and Vegetation Management, Section 5.3...""}, {""term"": ""lake clarity"", ""page"": 366, ""text"": ""...source contributions and transport pathways. DRI’s recommendations focus on further source assessment, transport analysis, evaluation of potential effects on lake clarity and drinking water, and continued assessment of strategies to reduce plastic entering the environment. Unlike disposable plastic bottles, outdoor plastic-c...""}, {""term"": ""stream environment zone"", ""page"": 366, ""text"": ""...ook chapter (Attachment 4) that encourages plastic free and biodegradable materials where feasible, especially in sensitive locations such as shore zones and stream environment zones (SEZs). The draft also recognizes that certain applications may require plastic materials and emphasizes inspection, prompt repair, and timely removal of temp...""}, {""term"": ""stream environment zone"", ""page"": 374, ""text"": ""...cern Buried or enclosed Not in sensitive areas Temporary Cost-effective, quality substitutes Higher Concern Exposed to sun, weather, or abrasion Shore zone or Stream Environment Zones (SEZ) Permanent or not maintained Necessary for safety, containment, or infrastructure 374""}, {""term"": ""stream environment zone"", ""page"": 381, ""text"": ""...ook. PW Wetland/SEZ delineation protection Orange safety fencing Tempoary Maybe Required in water board discretionary permits to delineate/protect wetlands and stream environment zones. Orange construction fencing is most commonly used and while the water board does not dictate what is to be used, there is not a good alternative known at th...""}, {""term"": ""stormwater"", ""page"": 95, ""text"": ""...Public Works Department and Engineering Division routinely manage a variety of capital improvement and infrastructure projects throughout the City, including stormwater, drainage, street, and other public improvement work. As part of this work, the City periodically receives Subdivision Map Act submittals and related documen...""}, {""term"": ""stormwater"", ""page"": 197, ""text"": ""...-046), with an amendment in 2022 for an additional $1,000,000 for this Project (Resolution 2022-066). The City also received LTRA funding for the Tahoe Valley Stormwater and Greenbelt Improvement Project, Phase 2, in 2024 for $725,000 to be used for construction (Resolution 2023-019). On May 5, 2026, City Council accepted $30...""}, {""term"": ""stormwater"", ""page"": 197, ""text"": ""...and TRPA plan to award $300,000 towards the City’s Drainage Master Plan Update/Areawide Facility Analyses, if accepted by future City Council action. The Stormwater Quality Improvement Committee (SWQIC) and Tahoe Watershed Improvement Group (TWIG) working groups convene several times throughout the year to discuss regiona...""}, {""term"": ""aquatic invasive species"", ""page"": 197, ""text"": ""...d community resilience while leveraging local and state investments. There are three categories of LTRA funding: Forest Health, Water Quality/Watersheds, and Aquatic Invasive Species (AIS). Public Works and Fire staff participate in the LTRA process for Water Quality/Watersheds and Forest Health categories. The City has previously rece...""}, {""term"": ""aquatic invasive species"", ""page"": 200, ""text"": ""...regional environmental improvement projects within the Lake Tahoe Basin. There are three categories of funding – Forest Health, Water Quality/Watersheds, and Aquatic Invasive Species (AIS). B. Working groups meet throughout the year to discuss regional issues and prioritize projects for each fiscal year of available LTRA funding. The ti...""}, {""term"": ""aquatic invasive species"", ""page"": 351, ""text"": ""...ation Awareness Program, or LEAP, for youth in South Lake Tahoe. Students will participate in hands-on lessons focused on freshwater science, microplastics, aquatic invasive species, watershed health, and environmental leadership. Youth 351""}, {""term"": ""glyphosate"", ""page"": 13, ""text"": ""...r a total contract amount not-to-exceed of $289,000. Public comment was heard from the following people: 1. Ed Mosur 12. Adopt Resolution Regarding Use of Glyphosate and Approve Letters to U.S. President, U.S. Senators, U.S. Representatives, U.S. Forest Service, and Tahoe Regional Planning Agency Action: Passed Resoluti...""}, {""term"": ""glyphosate"", ""page"": 13, ""text"": ""...ident, U.S. Senators, U.S. Representatives, U.S. Forest Service, and Tahoe Regional Planning Agency Action: Passed Resolution 2026-095 regarding the use of glyphosate and approving letters to U.S. President, U.S. Senators, U.S. Representatives, U.S. Forest Service, and Tahoe Regional Planning Agency. Public comment was he...""}, {""term"": ""water quality"", ""page"": 5, ""text"": ""...equested Action / Suggested Motions: Pass a Resolution authorizing staff to participate in the Lake Tahoe Restoration Act (LTRA) project prioritization in the water quality and watershed restoration working groups based on City Council approved Capital Improvement Project (CIP) list and strategic priorities. Responsible Staff Memb...""}, {""term"": ""water quality"", ""page"": 196, ""text"": ""...equested Action / Suggested Motions: Pass a Resolution authorizing staff to participate in the Lake Tahoe Restoration Act (LTRA) project prioritization in the water quality and watershed restoration working groups based on City Council approved Capital Improvement Project (CIP) list and strategic priorities. Responsible Staff Memb...""}, {""term"": ""water quality"", ""page"": 197, ""text"": ""...clarity, natural resources, and community resilience while leveraging local and state investments. There are three categories of LTRA funding: Forest Health, Water Quality/Watersheds, and Aquatic Invasive Species (AIS). Public Works and Fire staff participate in the LTRA process for Water Quality/Watersheds and Forest Health...""}]"
